# Welcome to Collimate

A **channel** is a canvas of stacks, and a stack is a column of **cells**.

A cell holds files. Edits and runs save recoverable *versions*, so you can
change the current files and return to an earlier result in the files pane's
History.

---

## Make a cell

Press **＋** in the top bar (or **Alt+N**) for a fresh, empty cell.

Or drag and drop files anywhere on the canvas — they land as a cell, ready to
work on.

---

## Commands

Press **Ctrl+K** (**⌘K** on a Mac) or **/** to open the **Commands pane**:
commands for the selected cell, its stack and the channel, and a jump to any
cell on the canvas. **Ctrl+P** finds a file in the selected cell.

---

## Do a run

Press **Run**, the play icon beside the selected cell's status light (or
**Alt+R**), to open the runners panel. Select a **runner** with ↑↓ or a click:
**Enter** or a double-click runs it in the current cell, and **Shift+Enter**
runs it in a new cell. A prompt can tell the agent to read the files already
in the cell.

Runners are made and changed in **Capabilities**: press the **Capabilities**
pill in the top bar, and its **Manage** face lists every runner with **Edit**,
**Duplicate** and **Delete**, and **Add runner** to make one.

---

## Duplicate & compare

Press **Alt+D** to duplicate a cell into a new stack beside it — then run
rivals side by side and see which comes out best. **Alt+V** fans a stack out,
every cell side by side (Alt+V or Esc collapses it).

---

## The overview

**Alt+A** zooms out to the overview: every cell as a card, oldest first. The
**Removed** shelf on its top edge keeps every cell you remove, ready to
restore. Open the **journal** to read a cell's makes, edits, runs, duplicates,
merges and splits, with arrows to the cells it came from and went to.

Nothing you try is lost: every run stays a version you can come back to.

---

## Launch: shell & VS Code

**Launch** runners start a program in a container, live on the cell's files.

**Launch › Terminal** — a real shell.

**VS Code** — code-server on port 8080, right inside the cell.

**Git Clone** — paste a repository URL as the prompt and it lands in the cell.

---

## Git: clone, update & push

**Git** runners bring a repository (GitHub, GitLab or any Git server) into a folder of the cell and send your edits back, with git on this machine.

**Git › Clone / Update** — name the repository in the prompt (its address, or owner/name with a GitHub or GitLab account bound); the first run clones its files (never its .git), later runs merge the remote's new commits into your edits.

**Git › Push** — sends the folder's edits as one commit. Private repositories, SSH and every push need a Git entry from Key manager, which also holds your commit name and email.

---

## Web & Extract

**Web** opens a live page inside a cell. Press Capture and what is on screen
becomes files in that cell.

**Extract** pulls structured JSON back out of whatever files a cell holds.

---

## Agents

The **Agent** runners work on the cell's files. Their **Surface** setting picks
where you work with one:

**Annotation** — you post messages to the run from the cell's status pane,
pointing at files and at elements inside them; it reads them, answers in place
and edits the cell. Send one as **interrupt** to cut in on what it is doing.

**Chat** — a conversation over the cell's files, editing them as it goes.

**CLI** — the agent harness's own terminal in a Docker container.

What you send while the agent starts waits for it. With
**Start from the selected file** on, a run uses the selected file — the open
file tab, else the one picked in the files pane — as part of its first turn,
and the runners panel says which file it will use.

To carry work to a later run, ask for a summary: the agent writes a
`.llm_summary` file at the cell root, and a later run picks up from it when you
ask.

---

## The Collimate helper

**Guide**, an Agent runner on the Chat surface, explains the workbench from its bundled
overview and philosophy, then consults the accepted handler catalog for file
types and authoring rules. It can explain a file check; it does not inspect
installed packages or list saved runners. The user chooses runners, adds
packages and accepts builds in the app.

---

## Keys & tips

Accounts live in **Key manager**, shared across channels. Select an account
in the runner editor, under **Capabilities › Manage**. Paste credentials,
verify them and discover models in Key manager; running a runner uses the
saved selection directly.

Delete this cell whenever you are done — it is just a cell.
